WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.992 --> 00:00:10.880
"Plant-Based Protein: Are Pea
and Soy Protein Isolates Harmful?"

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.720
So, are these plant-based
burgers healthy or not?

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.240
And the answer is…
compared to what?

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.920
Eating is kind of a zero-sum game;
every food has an opportunity cost.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.680
I mean, every time we
put something in our mouth

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.120
it’s a lost opportunity to put
something even healthier in our mouth.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.800
So, if you want to know
if something is healthy,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.240
you have to compare it to
what you’d be eating instead.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.720
So, for example,
are eggs healthy?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.320
Compared to a breakfast
link sausage? Yes!

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.440
But compared to oatmeal?
Not even close.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.520
But look, sausage is considered
a group 1 carcinogen.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.680
In other words, we know consumption
of processed meat causes cancer.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.640
Each 50-gram serving a day,
that’s a single breakfast link,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.800
was linked to an 18% higher
risk of colorectal cancer.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.319
So, the risk of getting colorectal
cancer eating one link a day

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:08.640
is about the same as the increased
risk of lung cancer you’d get

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.960
breathing secondhand smoke all
day living with a smoking spouse.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.320
So, compared to sausage,
eggs are healthy,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.440
but compared to oatmeal,
eggs are not.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.080
So, when it comes to Beyond Meat
and Impossible Burger,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.120
yeah, they may be better in
that they have less saturated fat,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.680
but, hey, you want
less saturated fat?

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.080
Plant-based meat
alternatives are no match

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.920
for unprocessed plant foods,
such as beans or lentils.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.840
And a bean burrito or lentil
soup could certainly fill

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.200
the same culinary niche
as a lunchtime burger.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.080
But if you are going to
have some kind of burger,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.280
it’s easy to argue that the
plant-based versions are healthier.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.200
There is a sodium issue, and
it’s not that much, if any, lower,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.120
in saturated fat, since
they use coconut oil,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.760
which is basically just
as bad as animal fat;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.800
there’s not much
advantage on that front.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.440
Though the total protein is
similar across the board,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.640
does this matter? Or Is there any
advantage to eating plant protein

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.600
over animal protein?
Let’s look at the association

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.280
between animal and plant
protein intake and mortality.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.400
In the twin Harvard cohorts,
following more than 100,000 men

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.440
and women over decades, “…after
adjusting for other dietary

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.160
and lifestyle factors, animal
protein intake was associated

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.160
with a higher risk [of] mortality,
particularly [dying from

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.520
cardiovascular disease], whereas
higher plant protein intake

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.160
was associated with 
[a] lower all-cause mortality”,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.240
meaning a lower risk of dying
from all causes put together.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.880
So, “replacing animal protein
of various origins

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.920
with plant protein was associated
with lower mortality”,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.680
especially if you’re replacing
processed meat and egg protein,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.680
which were the worst. But when
it comes to living a longer life,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.080
plant protein sources beat out
each and every animal protein source.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.520
Not just better
than bacon and eggs,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.680
but better than burgers, chicken,
turkey, fish, and dairy protein.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.160
Together with other studies, these
“findings support the importance

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.960
of protein sources for the
long-term health outcome

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.040
and suggest plants constitute
a preferred protein source

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.600
compared [to] animal foods.” Why?

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.960
Well, unlike animal protein, plant
protein has not been associated

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.040
with increased levels
of the cancer-promoting

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.880
growth hormone IGF-1, for example.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.760
Now, soy protein is similar
enough to animal protein

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.360
that at high enough doses, like eating
two Impossible Burgers a day,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.040
you may bump your IGF-1.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.320
But the only reason we care
about IGF-1 is cancer risk,

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.960
and if anything, higher soy
intake is associated with

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.440
a decreased risk of cancer.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.640
For example, a recent systematic
review and meta-analysis found

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.760
that soy protein intake was
associated with a decreased risk

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.360
in breast cancer mortality;
we’re talking “a 12 percent reduction

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.960
in breast cancer death
[associated with] each 5-gram-a-day

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.440
increase in soy protein intake.”

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.000
But the high soy groups
in these studies

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.720
were on the order of
more than 16 grams a day,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.440
associated with a
whopping 62% lower risk

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.880
of dying from breast cancer.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.840
More than 10 grams of soy
protein a day may be good,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.720
associated with cutting
breast cancer mortality risk

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.520
nearly in half, and getting
more than 16 grams a day

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.880
may be better, which is like
one Impossible Burger a day.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.720
But we simply don’t know what happens

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.840
at consumption levels far above that.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.040
Plant protein has also been
linked to lower blood pressure,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.320
reduced LDL cholesterol, and
improved insulin sensitivity.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.680
No wonder “substitution of
plant protein for animal protein

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.040
has been related to a lower incidence

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.320
of cardiovascular disease
and type 2 diabetes.”

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.880
Indeed, 21 different studies following
nearly a half million people,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.440
and “high… animal protein
intakes [were] associated with

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.960
an increased risk of [type 2 diabetes],

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.680
whereas [even just] moderate
plant protein intake

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.760
is associated with a decreased
risk of [type 2 diabetes].”

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.640
OK, but these were just
observational studies.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.680
They all tried to control for other
dietary and lifestyle factors,

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:58.240
but you can’t prove cause-and-effect,
until...you put it to the test.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.280
The “Effect of Replacing Animal
Protein with Plant Protein

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.720
on [blood sugar] Control in
Diabetes: A Systematic Review

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.600
and Meta-Analysis of
Randomized Controlled Trials.”

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.160
Even just switching out about
a third of your protein

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.760
from animal to plant sources
yielded significant improvements

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.280
in long-term blood sugar control,
and fasting blood sugars, and insulin.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.280
You can do the same thing
looking at cholesterol.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.160
Here’s a systematic review and
meta-analysis of randomized

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.880
controlled trials on the effect
of plant protein on blood fats.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.320
And indeed, swapping in plant
protein for animal protein

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.760
decreases LDL cholesterol,
and this benefit occurs

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.320
whether you start out at high
cholesterol or low cholesterol,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.360
whether you’re swapping out
dairy, or meat, and eggs,

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.240
and whether you’re swapping in
soy or other plant proteins.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.360
We’ve known about the beneficial
effects of soy on cholesterol

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.200
going back nearly 40 years, but
other sources of plant protein

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.400
can do it as well. Yeah, but
we’re not swapping beans for beef.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.320
These products are mostly
just isolated plant proteins,

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.840
mostly pea protein isolate
in the case of Beyond,

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.720
and concentrated soy protein
in the case of Impossible.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.440
If you just isolate out
the plant proteins themselves

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.480
are you still going to get benefits?

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.120
Yes, surprisingly. Check it out.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.320
Interestingly, the researchers
concluded, that they did not ﬁnd

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.360
a signiﬁcant difference between
protein isolate products

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.800
and whole food sources, “suggesting
that the cholesterol-lowering

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.640
effects are at least, in part, attributable

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.520
to the plant protein
itself rather than just

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.720
the associated nutrients.”

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.040
So, it’s not just because
plant protein travels

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.520
with fiber or less saturated fat.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.600
Plant proteins break down
into a different distribution

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.880
of amino acids; and so, it’s
like if you give people arginine,

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.800
an amino acid found
more in plant foods,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.280
that alone can bring
down people’s cholesterol.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.800
And even plant protein concentrates
used in these products

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.760
aren’t pure protein, retaining
a few active compounds

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.760
such as phytosterols and antioxidants,

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.746
which also can have beneficial effects.

